"""The Tides of Magic - narration v2 (Mirri Maz Duur guides; Doreah = second female voice).
Each snippet: (id, voice, direction, text). QUOTE placeholders are filled by Luke from his own edition."""

M, D = 'Mirri', 'Doreah'

CH = [
 ('1', 'Cold Open: The Legend', [
  ('01-1', D, 'jung, hell, verträumt wie ein Märchen', '[ZITAT Q1: Doreahs Legende, Text wie Snippet 01-1 im Tab English, A Game of Thrones, Kap. 23 (Daenerys III)]'),
  ('01-2', M, 'ruhig, erzählend', "A slave girl told that story to Daenerys Targaryen, on the long ride across the Dothraki Sea. She had it from a trader out of Qarth. A tale for children, passed from mouth to mouth across seas and centuries. No one believed it."),
  ('01-3', M, 'persönlicher, eine Spur leiser', "Neither did I. I was a godswife in Lhazar, and I learned my craft in Asshai, from the shadowbinders. I knew what magic could do. And how little of it was left."),
  ('01-4', M, 'langsam, schwer', "Then they tied me to a pyre, beside a dead khal. And a girl walked into the fire with me, and walked out with three dragons."),
  ('01-5', M, 'sehr ruhig, Pause nach „night“', "I burned that night. She did not. I have had a long time to wonder why."),
  ('01-6', M, 'wärmer, öffnet sich', "This book belonged to a man who watched the stars. He asked the question I could not answer. Why is magic coming back? And why everywhere at once, in the ice of the North and in the fire of the East?"),
  ('01-7', M, 'fest, Gewicht auf „low tide“', "His answer: it never left. It was only at low tide."),
 ]),
 ('2', 'The Riddle', [
  ('02-1', M, 'sachlich', "Let's start with what actually happened, in the order it happened."),
  ('02-2', M, 'unheimlich, gedämpft', "Far in the North, beyond the Wall, three men of the Night's Watch ride into the haunted forest. There they meet the Others, creatures that had been a children's story for thousands of years."),
  ('02-3', M, 'neutral', "Months later, far to the east, the girl walks into the pyre. Soon after, a red comet appears in the sky."),
  ('02-4', M, 'Tempo anziehen, dann persönlich bei „Marwyn“', "Then it breaks out everywhere. In Oldtown, in the Citadel, a candle of black glass begins to burn. It burns in the rooms of a maester I knew. In Asshai, Marwyn taught me how a body is put together, and the Common Tongue I am speaking now."),
  ('02-5', M, 'zügig, Aufzählung, „Six times“ betont', "In King's Landing, the pyromancers find their old spells for wildfire suddenly working better. Thoros of Myr, a red priest who lived on cheap tricks, brings a dead man back to life. Not once. Six times. And the red woman, Melisandre, feels her power grow."),
  ('02-6', M, 'nachdenklich', "Everyone has an explanation. When a young man of the Citadel asked Marwyn what fed the flame, he answered with a question."),
  ('Q2', M, 'liest vor, etwas langsamer', '[ZITAT Q2: Marwyns Antwort auf die Frage, was die Flamme nährt, A Feast for Crows, Kap. 45 (Samwell V)]'),
  ('02-7', M, 'nachdenklich', "His answer, and most people's: the dragons are back, so magic is back. Others say fire woke the ice. Or ice woke the fire."),
  ('02-8', M, 'bestimmt', "But look at the order. The Others were moving before a single egg had hatched. The dragons cannot have woken them. And nowhere is there a sign that the ice woke the dragons."),
  ('02-9', M, 'ruhig, Schlusssatz mit Gewicht', "When the water rises on every shore at once, it isn't one wave pushing the next. It's the tide coming in."),
 ]),
 ('3', 'The Tide', [
  ('03-1', M, 'warm, wie eine Erinnerung', "In the North, an old woman tells a boy stories of the Long Night, and tells him what he is."),
  ('Q3', M, 'liest vor, wie eine alte Frau, ohne zu verstellen', '[ZITAT Q3: Old Nan zu Bran, „summer child … what do you know of fear?“, A Game of Thrones, Kap. 24 (Bran IV)]'),
  ('03-2', M, 'ruhig', "A child who has only known summer cannot imagine winter. When it comes to magic, everyone in this world is a summer child. Born at the low point of an age, they think the world has always been this way."),
  ('03-3', M, 'These: langsam, klar, jedes Satzende fest', "Here is what the stargazer wrote. Magic is not a switch, on or off. It is a field that runs through the whole world. And it rises and falls, like the sea. Only its rhythm is counted in thousands of years."),
  ('03-4', M, 'präzise', "It is not a gentle wave. The rise comes suddenly, within a few years. The fall drags on for millennia. Less a wave than the edge of a saw."),
  ('03-5', M, 'getragen, groß', "The last peak was the Long Night. Not a war that happened to meet a cruel winter, but the height of a magical age. The Others were never stronger. And neither were men. The Long Night falls in the Age of Heroes. Remember that."),
  ('03-6', M, 'sachlich', "When was it? The maesters say six or eight thousand years ago, and some chronicles count thousands of years fewer. The stargazer puts the peak between four and eight thousand years before the dragons came back."),
  ('03-7', M, 'leise', "Since then, the field has been falling. So slowly that no one notices. And just before the girl walked into the fire, it reached the bottom."),
 ]),
 ('4', 'The Broken Sky', [
  ('04-1', M, 'Frage, neugierig', "Why does the field rise? And why so fast?"),
  ('04-2', M, 'erzählend, „A cause“ fest', "When the red comet appeared, everyone read it as a sign. For the dragons, for a king, for a war. The stargazer saw something else. Not a sign. A cause."),
  ('04-3', M, 'staunend, weit', "Picture an enormous body in the heavens that shattered long ago. Its pieces travel together, a field of debris on a vast path. Every few thousand years it passes close to the world, and brings the power those pieces carry. The comet is only the piece we happen to see."),
  ('04-4', M, 'ruhig', "Remember the legend. A moon that broke apart. It isn't a nursery tale. It is a memory, worn thin by retelling. And some passes leave more than light. Pieces fall."),
  ('04-5', M, 'Zeugnis, nah, leise', "I lived in Asshai. The whole city is built of a black, oily stone that seems to drink the light. No one knows who raised it. Its own people say a city has stood there since the world began."),
  ('04-6', M, 'Aufzählung, Bilder tragen', "The same stone stands in the ruins of Yeen, deep in the jungles of Sothoryos. On Pyke, the Ironborn sit on the Seastone Chair, carved from oily black stone no one can name. In Oldtown, the Hightower rests on a black fortress older than any record."),
  ('04-7', M, 'geheimnisvoll', "And in the far east, the histories of Yi Ti remember the Bloodstone Emperor."),
  ('Q4', M, 'liest vor', '[ZITAT Q4: der Bloodstone Emperor stürzt die Götter und betet einen schwarzen Stein an, der vom Himmel fiel, The World of Ice & Fire, „The Bones and Beyond: Yi Ti“]'),
  ('04-8', M, 'Schlussfolgerung, fest', "These stones come from different ages, in places that have nothing to do with one another, some among peoples whose names are lost. That only makes sense if they fell over many passes, across many cycles. None of this is happening for the first time."),
  ('04-9', M, 'leiser, als würde sie umblättern', "There is a note in the margin here, in his hand. I will read it as he wrote it."),
  ('04-10', M, 'direkt, persönlich, normales Sprechtempo', "While working on this, I found that the second moon and the black stones have inspired countless theories, and many people, me included, came to their ideas on their own. What I couldn't find, at least nowhere I looked, was this exact theory: a tide of magic that rises and falls with the fragments of one shattered body. I don't want to take credit for anyone else's work. If an article or a video made the same argument before this one, point me to it in the comments. I'll mention it, and I'll credit it."),
 ]),
 ('5', 'The Long Ebb', [
  ('05-1', M, 'erzählend', "If the Long Night was the peak, everything after it is the long fall. And some of this world's great mysteries fall into line."),
  ('05-2', M, 'kalt, weit', "The Wall. Raised, the stories say, with spells woven into the ice, to keep the Others out. It has stood for thousands of years. But mostly because the thing it was built against kept growing weaker."),
  ('05-3', M, 'Schlusssatz langsam', "By now the Night's Watch is a shadow of itself, and almost no one believes in the Others. Then the old enemy returns, just as the Wall is at its weakest. A wall can hold back a flood. It cannot hold back winter."),
  ('05-4', M, 'groß, stolz', "Valyria. The mightiest empire this world has ever seen, built on dragons, blood and sorcery. At its heart, the Fourteen Flames, a chain of burning mountains."),
  ('05-5', M, 'sachlich', "Why did it fall? Even scholars of this world had a suspicion."),
  ('Q5', M, 'liest vor', '[ZITAT Q5: Septon Barth und einige Maester: die Zauber, die die Vierzehn Flammen bändigten, versagten, The World of Ice & Fire, „Ancient History: The Doom of Valyria“]'),
  ('05-6', M, 'dunkel, „tear open“ hart', "The stargazer adds why they failed. Not at the lowest point, but when the field fell below what such binding needs. About four hundred years before the dragons came back, the spells break. And the mountains tear open."),
  ('05-7', M, 'knapp', "Valyrian steel was lost with Valyria, they say. But that steel needs spells and dragonfire. What vanished was not the knowledge. It was the possibility."),
  ('05-8', M, 'traurig', "After the Doom, only the Targaryen dragons remain, and in the Dragonpit they never grow as large as their ancestors. The last one dies in the year 153 after the Conquest."),
  ('Q8', M, 'liest vor, traurig', '[ZITAT Q8: die letzte Drachin, klein, verkümmert, mit welken Flügeln, The Hedge Knight]'),
  ('05-9', M, 'steigernd, jeder Satz ein Schlag', "Then, a century and a half of failure. Aegon the Third brings nine mages across the sea. Baelor prays over the eggs. Aegon the Fourth builds dragons of wood and iron. Aerion Brightflame drinks wildfire, and burns. At Summerhall, the last attempt ends in flames."),
  ('05-10', M, 'Zeugnis, ganz ruhig', "Then a pyre, and a girl who walks out of it with three dragons. Not because she knew more than all of them. Not because her blood was purer. I was on that pyre. She walked into the fire at the right moment."),
  ('05-11', M, 'nachdenklich, Schluss leise', "The same pattern runs through the giants, once a people, now a remnant dying out beyond the Wall. And through men. The legends tell of kings from the Age of Heroes who ruled for centuries. The maesters call it exaggeration. The stargazer called it a description. What walks the world now are the withered heirs of the Long Night."),
 ]),
 ('6', 'Life of Ice, Life of Fire', [
  ('06-1', M, 'Frage', "If ice and fire don't wake each other, what are they?"),
  ('06-2', M, 'klar', "The stargazer's answer: not elements, and not gods. Foundations of life. The field has two sides, and on each side there is life that draws from it."),
  ('06-3', M, 'kalt, präzise', "The Others are made of ice. Their bodies, their strength, their sorcery. They are neither walking corpses nor a storm. A ranger of the Night's Watch heard one speak."),
  ('Q6', M, 'liest vor, kalt', '[ZITAT Q6: die Stimme des Anderen wie brechendes Eis auf einem See, A Game of Thrones, Prolog]'),
  ('06-4', M, 'ernst', "They wear armor, carry blades, and laugh. They take Craster's sons beyond the Wall. That is a people, with its own customs, and perhaps its own claim to this world. For thousands of years it has watched its power drain away. Now it feels that power returning. The Others don't summon winter. It is coming anyway. But they ride it."),
  ('06-5', M, 'erklärend', "On the other side stands fire. Ice spreads like a flood, bound to land and weather. Fire lives in blood: in dragons, in Valyrian lines, in priests. That is why history remembers them so differently."),
  ('06-6', M, 'trocken, letzter Satz hart', "Against the ice, men built a wall. Against fire, they built weapons. Against a flood you raise a dike. Against a dragon, only a spear will do. In Dorne, a single bolt took the dragon Meraxes through the eye."),
  ('06-7', M, 'Zeugnis, leise, stolz', "Men belong to the side of fire. I know it in my own blood. I sang over a dying khal, and the old powers answered me. That was the year the tide turned."),
  ('06-8', M, 'warm', "Something wakes in the Starks as well. No direwolf had been seen south of the Wall in two hundred years. Then six pups find six children, and the children begin to see through their wolves' eyes. Which side that gift draws from, no one can say. But it wakes now, with everything else."),
  ('06-9', M, 'groß, ruhig', "In a magical age, not one side grows strong. Both do. The Long Night was not good against evil. It was two kinds of life outgrowing themselves at the same moment. And colliding."),
 ]),
 ('7', 'Older Than Valyria', [
  ('07-1', M, 'Frage', "Where do dragons come from? The scholars of this world have argued about it for centuries."),
  ('07-2', M, 'sachlich', "Septon Barth, Hand to a Targaryen king, had his own answer."),
  ('Q7', M, 'liest vor', '[ZITAT Q7: Barth: die Valyrer schufen Drachen mit Blutmagie aus Wyvern, The World of Ice & Fire, „Beyond the Free Cities: Sothoryos“]'),
  ('07-3', M, 'Gegenargument', "The maesters object. Ancient dragon bones have been found from Ib in the far north to the jungles of Sothoryos. How could Valyria have invented something older than itself?"),
  ('07-4', M, 'fest, kurz', "The stargazer's answer: they are both right."),
  ('07-5', M, 'erzählend', "The old bones belong to the dragons of an earlier age. When that age ebbed, they withered, like everything that leans hard on the field. What was left survived where the world itself still burned: deep inside the volcanoes."),
  ('07-6', M, 'steigend', "Then came the Long Night, and the high tide with it. The Valyrians were still shepherds then. In the Fourteen Flames they found what remained of the old dragons. And with the strength of a rising field, and with blood, they shaped the dragons of their age."),
  ('07-7', M, 'Zeugnis, dunkel', "I know what blood can buy. I traded a stallion's death for a khal's life, and I know what it cost. Valyria paid that kind of price for thousands of years."),
  ('07-8', M, 'Schlag', "They didn't invent dragons out of nothing. They brought them back."),
  ('07-9', M, 'ruhig, Schluss leise', "That is why Valyria's dragons stayed mighty while the field was already falling. They were made at the peak, with the reserves of a whole age. And they shared the fate of everything born at the peak. They went out with the tide."),
 ]),
 ('8', 'The Objections', [
  ('08-1', M, 'sachlich, fast amüsiert', "A theory is only as good as its answers to the hardest objections. The stargazer wrote down four."),
  ('08-2', M, 'Karte vorlesen, dann antworten', "First. The dragons died out for ordinary reasons. The Dance of the Dragons, the Targaryen civil war, killed the greatest of them. True. But that doesn't explain why the survivors stayed stunted, or why for a century and a half not one egg hatched. The war thinned the dragons. The ebb explains why they never recovered."),
  ('08-3', M, 'Karte vorlesen, dann antworten', "Second. A rhythm needs more than one beat, and we know of only one Long Night. The black stones answer that. They come from different ages. If they are pieces of the shattered body, every stone marks an earlier pass. So do the bones older than Valyria. Perhaps there weren't too few passes. Perhaps too few were written down."),
  ('08-4', M, 'ehrlich, zögernd', "Third. Some magic fits neither ice nor fire. The greenseers. The weirwoods. The Faceless Men of Braavos. Here the stargazer was least sure himself. There may be more than two sources. Or a fragment lies hidden beneath Braavos. It remains a guess."),
  ('08-5', M, 'fair, dann fest', "Fourth. A theory that explains everything explains nothing. Fair. So here is what would break it. If the Long Night was set off by one single act, a pact, a spell, a betrayal, then it was an event, not a rhythm, and he was wrong. Until then, it is the answer that ties the most loose ends together without cutting any of them."),
 ]),
 ('9', 'The Warning', [
  ('09-1', M, 'ruhig', "The slave girl's story has a second part that everyone forgets."),
  ('09-2', D, 'wie 01-1, leiser, fast ein Flüstern', '[ZITAT Q9: der zweite Teil der Legende, A Game of Thrones, Kap. 23 (Daenerys III), Doreah]'),
  ('09-3', M, 'getragen', "The stargazer believed this world is living through exactly that. Not the end of the world. The beginning of a new age. The field is breaking open."),
  ('09-4', M, 'zwei gleich gebaute Sätze', "Ice is returning, and with it a people that has waited thousands of years. Fire is returning, in dragons, in priests, in the blood of old houses."),
  ('09-5', M, 'fest', "No one can stop the tide itself. No wall, no sword, no hero. But the tide lifts both sides. Who survives the rise, and at what price, is not decided by the sky. It is decided by those who live beneath it."),
  ('09-6', M, 'leise, eindringlich', "Maybe that is the real warning. Not that the Long Night is coming, but that no one remembers it has been here before. The bones lie in the sand, the stones in the foundations of cities, and everyone calls them legends. This is what a world looks like after thousands of years at low tide."),
  ('09-7', M, 'fast geflüstert', "We are all summer children."),
  ('09-8', M, 'Abschied, warm', "I burned at the turning of the tide. He is still out there, watching the sky."),
  ('09-9', M, 'freundlich, direkt', "So what do you think? Is magic in this world a rhythm, or an event? Leave your answer below. He reads them all."),
 ]),
]

if __name__ == '__main__':
    WPM = 140
    tot_w = tot_c = 0
    for n, title, sn in CH:
        w = sum(len(t.split()) for _, v, _, t in sn if not t.startswith('['))
        c = sum(len(t) for _, v, _, t in sn if not t.startswith('['))
        tot_w += w; tot_c += c
        print(f'Ch {n:>2} {title:28s} {w:4d} words  {c:5d} chars  ~{w / WPM:4.1f} min  max snippet {max(len(t) for *_, t in sn)}')
    print(f'TOTAL {tot_w} words, {tot_c} chars (+ ~320 for the two Doreah lines) -> {tot_w / WPM:.1f} min speech at {WPM} wpm')
